WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.700
Database connections are often pooled, but pooling does not remove lifetime responsibility.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:11.000
A connection that is not closed or disposed correctly may stay checked out longer than intended.

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:19.900
Under pressure, that can affect other requests, background jobs, migrations, reports, and operational tools that need database capacity.

4
00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:29.140
Connections, commands, readers, transactions, and data contexts should be released according to ownership and framework guidance.

5
00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:33.640
A data reader may keep a connection busy while rows are being consumed.

6
00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:40.300
A transaction may hold locks until it is committed, rolled back, or disposed.

7
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.360
A context may track entities and hold state that is not safe to share broadly.

8
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:49.880
Long-running transactions increase operational risk.

9
00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:58.380
They can hold locks, block other work, increase rollback impact, complicate error recovery, and make production incidents harder to reason about.

10
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:04.640
Transaction boundaries should be narrow, purposeful, and aligned with the consistency requirement of the operation.

11
00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.320
Dependency injection lifetimes matter.

12
00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:14.860
Singleton, scoped, and transient services have different expectations for resource ownership and thread safety.

13
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:25.280
DbContext and similar scoped services should not become shared singletons, and they should not be used concurrently unless the framework and application design explicitly support that pattern.

14
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:30.200
Background work needs special care because it often runs outside the request scope.

15
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:37.260
A queued operation or hosted service should not accidentally capture request-scoped services beyond their intended lifetime.

16
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:46.000
It should create the scope it needs, handle cleanup predictably, and separate runtime, migration, administrative, and reporting access where appropriate.
